    {
      "id": "claim_809ea040ba724ed19ea3c7be276a89fe",
      "statement": "The signed probe is liveness and identity: a installing signer at boot, a peer GET /peer/probe with a random challenge signed by the fleet MAC, and the node responds with its advertise address and challenge signature; probe refuses a node with no signed-probe key and refuses a node whose peer protocol is not the current version.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/peer_probe.rs#L103-L135",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e464006754200c3ccce10fbd47f71bda82fe977d24c35c3b446f3a18e5b7209b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/peer_probe.rs#L18-L60",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c3690bad01bea44de74e536c7fdf460718b26b624475f99823bc712afa3fcf5e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_265d1a867b07465ca9963f96182d3bf2",
      "statement": "The peer tunnel is an h1 upgrade on /peer/tunnel (CONNECT mechanics with Upgrade): after 101 the connection is an opaque duplex stream and app requests cross as literal HTTP driven by hyper on both ends — the hop never interprets inner bytes so header fidelity is preserved (Host, Content-Length, Upgrade are data) — and per-call control (scope, cell name, request id, capacity handoff) rides x-cells-* inner headers that node A overwrites and node B strips so reserved names cannot be smuggled by an application.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/main/peer_tunnel.rs#L3-L27",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:bdca9083f53efd5f7410d2e89ec7b395d5952b3e3b14067582ae0c57c79bd69a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_0df151cce5f34145beeded98bb5638af",
      "statement": "Tunnel protocol identification: TUNNEL_PROTOCOL = celld-tunnel, TUNNEL_VERSION = 5, with x-cells-tunnel-version, x-cells-tunnel-kind, x-cells-scope, x-cells-do-name, x-cells-request-id, and x-cells-capacity-handoff as the reserved inner headers.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/main/peer_tunnel.rs#L40-L52",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a85f07077a8e987b2014960f975b07947549b6fc603f2a35846b9297edf5c217:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a09a4e5cea0449a0adf773b7ca4d62eb",
      "statement": "Idle tunnels are pooled per peer and reused via h1 keep-alive rather than fresh TCP per call, because without reuse a loopback-speed caller saturates the ephemeral port range into TIME_WAIT within seconds (about 1000 calls/s is the hard OS ceiling).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/main/peer_tunnel.rs#L22-L33",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f9c3305566f0e67a06400e9c2d022f91b92803e2508b943079a1ae828d6bc77f:"
        }
      ]
    },
